Ladybird’s UI process now opens a file a renderer asks for only once
the path, with every symbolic link resolved, is outside /proc, /sys,
and /dev, and only if the file it opens is a regular file or a
directory on a file system other than procfs or sysfs.

Chromium never hands a renderer a descriptor for a file: URL. Its
FileURLLoader (content/browser/loader/file_url_loader_factory.cc) reads
the file in the browser process and streams the bytes over a Mojo data
pipe, and ChildProcessSecurityPolicyImpl limits file: requests to the
processes it granted that scheme. And Chrome’s own IsAccessAllowed()
(chrome/browser/net/chrome_network_delegate.cc) puts no limit on the
path on desktop Linux, so it shows a /proc file the address bar names;
Ladybird deliberately refuses those, since a navigation that a page
starts goes through the same broker in Ladybird.

Firefox’s Linux sandbox broker opens paths for a content process, but
its SandboxBrokerClient::DoCall() rewrites /proc/self/ to the requesting
process’s own pid before the request leaves it, so “self” always means
the content process itself, never the parent.

These comparisons come from reading the engines’ sources, not from
running them: macOS has no /proc to compare against.
